ss="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Thaya (Czech Dyje) is a river in Austria and the Czech Republic, about 235 kilometres long.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/theagenes-of-megara"><span class="enc-name">Theagenes of Megara</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theagenes was tyrant of Megara in Greece in the second half of the seventh century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theatre"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theatre.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theatre</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Theatre is the performance of drama before an audience, and also the buildings designed for it.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/thebaid"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/thebaid.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Thebaid</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Thebaid was the southern region of ancient Egypt around Thebes, stretching from about Abydos to the First Cataract at Aswan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/thebe-of-pherae"><span class="enc-name">Thebe of Pherae</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Thebe of Pherae was a daughter of Jason of Pherae and the wife of Alexander, tyrant of Pherae in Thessaly.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/thebes"><span class="enc-name">Thebes</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Thebes was an ancient city in Upper Egypt on the Nile, the site of present-day Luxor.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/thebes-greece.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Thebes, Greece</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Thebes was the leading city of Boeotia in central Greece and the dominant member of the Boeotian League.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/thefarie-velianas"><span class="enc-name">Thefarie Velianas</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Thefarie Velianas was a ruler of the Etruscan city of Caere around 500 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/thein-sein"><span class="enc-name">Thein Sein</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Thein Sein is a Myanmar general and politician who was prime minister from 2007 and president from 2011 to 2016.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aethelbald-of-mercia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aethelbald-of-mercia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Æthelbald of Mercia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Æthelbald was king of Mercia from 716 to 757 and the dominant ruler of southern England for much of his reign.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aethelberht-ii-of-east-anglia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aethelberht-ii-of-east-anglia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Æthelberht II of East Anglia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Æthelberht II was king of East Anglia in the late 8th century, known from a few coins and later accounts.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aethelberht-of-kent"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aethelberht-of-kent.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Æthelberht of Kent</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Æthelberht was king of Kent from about 589 to 616 and the first English king to accept Christianity.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aethelflaed"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aethelflaed.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Æthelflæd</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Æthelflæd, Lady of the Mercians, was the eldest daughter of Alfred the Great and ruled English Mercia from the death of her husband Æthelred in 911 until her own death…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aethelfrith-of-northumbria"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aethelfrith-of-northumbria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Æthelfrith of Northumbria</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Æthelfrith was king of Bernicia from about 593 and, from about 604, also of Deira, uniting them as the first ruler of Northumbria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-i-of-wessex"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aethelred-i-of-wessex.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Æthelred I of Wessex</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Æthelred I was king of Wessex from 865 to 871, the fourth son of Æthelwulf to reign.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-of-mercia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aethelred-of-mercia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Æthelred of Mercia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Æthelred was king of Mercia from 675 to 704, a son of Penda.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-the-unready"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aethelred-the-unready.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Æthelred the Unready</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Æthelred the Unready was king of the English from 978 to 1016, apart from a brief exile in 1013–1014; his nickname means 'poorly advised'.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-lord-of-the-mercians"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aethelred-lord-of-the-mercians.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Æthelred, Lord of the Mercians</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Æthelred was the ruler of English Mercia from about 881 until his death in 911, after the Danes had taken the eastern half of the kingdom.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aethelstan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Æthelstan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Æthelstan was king of the Anglo-Saxons from 924 and the first king of all England from 927, when he took York and received the submission of northern rulers.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aethelswith"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aethelswith.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Æthelswith</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Æthelswith was the daughter of King Æthelwulf of Wessex and sister of Alfred the Great.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aethelthryth"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aethelthryth.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Æthelthryth</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Æthelthryth, also known as Etheldreda or Audrey, was an East Anglian princess, a daughter of King Anna.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/oethelwald-of-deira"><span class="enc-name">Œthelwald of Deira</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Œthelwald of Deira was king of Deira from about 651 to 655, a son of Oswald of Northumbria, appointed under his uncle Oswiu.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aethelwealh-of-sussex"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aethelwealh-of-sussex.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Æthelwealh of Sussex</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Æthelwealh was king of the South Saxons in the later 7th century and the first Christian king of Sussex.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aethelwold-aetheling"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aethelwold-aetheling.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Æthelwold ætheling</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Æthelwold was a West Saxon prince, the son of King Æthelred I and cousin of Edward the Elder.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aethelwulf-of-wessex"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aethelwulf-of-wessex.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Æthelwulf of Wessex</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Æthelwulf was king of Wessex from 839 to 858, the son of Ecgberht.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/thembu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/thembu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Thembu</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Thembu are a Xhosa-speaking Nguni people of the Eastern Cape, South Africa, whose homeland, Thembuland, lies around the Mbashe and Mthatha rivers.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theme-system"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theme-system.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theme system</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The theme system was the military and administrative organisation of the Byzantine Empire from around the seventh century, in which provinces called themes were…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/themis"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/themis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Themis</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Themis was the Greek goddess of divine law, order and custom, a Titan and daughter of Gaia and Uranus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/themistocles"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/themistocles.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Themistocles</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Themistocles was an Athenian statesman and general who persuaded Athens to build a large fleet with the silver of Laurion.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/themistokli-germenji"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/themistokli-germenji.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Themistokli Gërmenji</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Themistokli Gërmenji (1871–1917) was an Albanian nationalist from Korçë who led guerrilla bands against Ottoman and Greek forces.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/themistoklis-sofoulis"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/themistoklis-sofoulis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Themistoklis Sofoulis</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Themistoklis Sofoulis was a Greek politician from Samos.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/theo-van-gogh-film-director"><span class="enc-name">Theo van Gogh (film director)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theo van Gogh was a Dutch film director, writer and provocative columnist, a great-grandson of Theo van Gogh, brother of the painter Vincent.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/theobald-bourke-1st-viscount-mayo"><span class="enc-name">Theobald Bourke, 1st Viscount Mayo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theobald Bourke, known as Tibbot na Long ('of the ships'), was the son of Grace O'Malley and Richard an Iarainn Bourke, said to have been born at sea.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/theobald-i-of-blois"><span class="enc-name">Theobald I of Blois</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theobald I of Blois (died 975), known as the Trickster, was a vassal of Hugh the Great who by about 940 held the counties of Blois, Chartres and Tours.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theobald-i-of-navarre"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theobald-i-of-navarre.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theobald I of Navarre</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theobald I was king of Navarre from 1234 and, as Theobald IV, count of Champagne from his birth in 1201.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theobald-ii-count-of-champagne"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theobald-ii-count-of-champagne.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theobald II, Count of Champagne</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theobald II, called the Great, was count of Champagne from 1125 and, as Theobald IV, count of Blois and Chartres from 1102.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/theobald-walter"><span class="enc-name">Theobald Walter</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theobald Walter was an Anglo-Norman lord who came to Ireland with Prince John in 1185 and was granted lands in Munster and the hereditary office of butler of Ireland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theobald-wolfe-tone"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theobald-wolfe-tone.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theobald Wolfe Tone</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theobald Wolfe Tone was an Irish revolutionary, a Protestant lawyer who helped found the Society of United Irishmen in 1791.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/theocles"><span class="enc-name">Theocles</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theocles was the leader of the Greek colonists from Chalcis in Euboea who founded Naxos around 734 BCE, according to Thucydides the first Greek colony in Sicily.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/theocracy"><span class="enc-name">Theocracy</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Theocracy is government in the name of God, by priests, a sacred ruler or religious law.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theocritus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theocritus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theocritus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theocritus was a Greek poet from Sicily, in Magna Graecia, and the creator of ancient Greek pastoral poetry.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodelinda"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theodelinda.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theodelinda</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodelinda was a Bavarian princess who became queen of the Lombards by marrying King Authari in 589 and, after his death, King Agilulf.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodemir-visigoth"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theodemir-visigoth.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theodemir (Visigoth)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodemir was a Visigothic noble who governed south-eastern Spain at the time of the Muslim conquest.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theoderich-von-treyden"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theoderich-von-treyden.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theoderich von Treyden</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theoderich von Treyden was a Cistercian monk and missionary in Livonia and the first abbot of Dünamünde near Riga.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodor-herzl"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theodor-herzl.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theodor Herzl</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodor Herzl was a Jewish journalist and writer born in Budapest and active in Vienna, regarded as the founder of modern political Zionism.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodor-leutwein"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theodor-leutwein.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theodor Leutwein</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodor Leutwein was a German officer who led the colony of South West Africa from 1894, as governor from 1898 to 1905.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodora-wife-of-justinian-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theodora-wife-of-justinian-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theodora (wife of Justinian I)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodora was a Byzantine empress, wife of Emperor Justinian I and one of the most influential women in Byzantine history.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodora-wife-of-theophilos"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theodora-wife-of-theophilos.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theodora (wife of Theophilos)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodora was a Byzantine empress, wife of Emperor Theophilos, who ruled as regent for their young son Michael III from 842 to 856.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodora-megale-komnene"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theodora-megale-komnene.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theodora Megale Komnene</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Despina Khatun was the name borne by Theodora Komnene, daughter of Emperor John IV of Trebizond, who married the Aq Qoyunlu ruler Uzun Hasan around 1458 as part of an…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodora-porphyrogenita"><span class="enc-name">Theodora Porphyrogenita</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodora Porphyrogenita was Byzantine empress from 1042 until her death in 1056, and sole ruler from 1055, the last sovereign of the Macedonian dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodore-i-laskaris"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theodore-i-laskaris.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theodore I Laskaris</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodore I Laskaris was the founder of the Empire of Nicaea, ruling from 1205 and as crowned emperor from 1208 until 1221.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodore-i-palaiologos"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theodore-i-palaiologos.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theodore I Palaiologos</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodore I Palaiologos was the Byzantine Despot of the Morea from 1383 to 1407, a son of Emperor John V.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodore-ii-laskaris"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theodore-ii-laskaris.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theodore II Laskaris</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodore II Laskaris was emperor of Nicaea from 1254 to 1258, the son of John III Doukas Vatatzes and grandson of Theodore I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodore-ii-muzaka"><span class="enc-name">Theodore II Muzaka</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodore II Muzaka (died 1389) was an Albanian lord of the Muzaka family who held Berat and the surrounding region of central Albania in the late 14th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodore-komnenos-doukas"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theodore-komnenos-doukas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theodore Komnenos Doukas</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodore Komnenos Doukas (c. 1180–c. 1253) was ruler of Epirus from 1215 who took Thessalonica from the Latins in 1224 and had himself crowned emperor.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodore-roosevelt"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theodore-roosevelt.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theodore Roosevelt</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodore Roosevelt was the 26th president of the United States, from 1901 to 1909.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodore-rshtuni"><span class="enc-name">Theodore Rshtuni</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodore Rshtuni (died 655) was an Armenian prince and military commander who led the defence of Armenia against the first Arab raids.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodore-svetoslav"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theodore-svetoslav.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theodore Svetoslav</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodore Svetoslav was tsar of Bulgaria from 1300 to 1322, son of George I Terter.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodore-von-neuhoff"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theodore-von-neuhoff.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theodore von Neuhoff</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodore von Neuhoff was a Westphalian adventurer who was crowned king of Corsica in April 1736 by islanders in revolt against Genoa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theodoric-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theodoric I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodoric I was king of the Visigoths from 418 to 451, ruling the federate kingdom that Rome had settled in Aquitaine, with Toulouse as its capital.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-ii"><span class="enc-name">Theodoric II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodoric II was king of the Visigoths from 453 to 466, having taken power by killing his brother Thorismund.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-ii-king-of-the-visigoths"><span class="enc-name">Theodoric II, King of the Visigoths</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodoric II was king of the Visigoths from 453 to 466, after murdering his brother Thorismund.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-the-great"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theodoric-the-great.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theodoric the Great</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodoric the Great was king of the Ostrogoths who invaded Italy at the request of Emperor Zeno, defeated Odoacer and ruled the peninsula from Ravenna from 493 to 526.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodoros-kolokotronis"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theodoros-kolokotronis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theodoros Kolokotronis</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodoros Kolokotronis was the leading military commander of the Greek War of Independence in the Peloponnese.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodoros-negris"><span class="enc-name">Theodoros Negris</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodoros Negris (1790–1824) was a Greek scholar and diplomat from Constantinople who served the Ottoman administration before joining the Greek War of Independence.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodoros-pangalos"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theodoros-pangalos.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theodoros Pangalos</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodoros Pangalos was a Greek general who seized power in a coup in June 1925 and ruled the Second Hellenic Republic as dictator until August 1926.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodorus-of-cyrene"><span class="enc-name">Theodorus of Cyrene</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodorus of Cyrene was a Greek mathematician of the 5th century BCE, a teacher of Plato and of Theaetetus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodorus-of-samos"><span class="enc-name">Theodorus of Samos</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodorus of Samos was a Greek architect, sculptor and metalworker of the 6th century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theodosius-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theodosius I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodosius I was a Roman emperor who died in 395 CE, the last to rule both the eastern and western halves of the empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theodosius-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theodosius II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodosius II was emperor of the Eastern Roman Empire from 408 to 450, succeeding his father Arcadius as a child.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-of-tarnovo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theodosius-of-tarnovo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theodosius of Tarnovo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodosius of Tarnovo was a Bulgarian monk and leading teacher of hesychasm, the Orthodox tradition of contemplative prayer.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theodulf-of-orleans"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theodulf-of-orleans.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theodulf of Orléans</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theodulf of Orléans was a scholar, poet and bishop at the court of Charlemagne, of Visigothic origin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theog"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theog.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theog</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Theog is a town in Shimla district of Himachal Pradesh, India, in the hills north-east of Shimla.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theognis-of-megara"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theognis-of-megara.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theognis of Megara</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theognis of Megara was a Greek elegiac poet of the sixth century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/theognostus-metropolitan-of-kiev"><span class="enc-name">Theognostus, Metropolitan of Kiev</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theognostus was a Greek churchman appointed by Constantinople as Metropolitan of Kiev and All Rus', an office he held from 1328 until his death in 1353.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theogony"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theogony.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theogony</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Theogony is a Greek poem by Hesiod, composed around 700 BCE, that tells the origin and genealogy of the gods.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/theoktistos-bryennios"><span class="enc-name">Theoktistos Bryennios</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theoktistos Bryennios was a Byzantine general and strategos of the theme of the Peloponnese in the 840s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theophanes-the-confessor"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theophanes-the-confessor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theophanes the Confessor</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theophanes the Confessor was a Byzantine monk and chronicler whose Chronographia covers the history of the empire from 284 to 813.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theophanes-the-greek"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theophanes-the-greek.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theophanes the Greek</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theophanes the Greek was a Byzantine painter of icons and frescoes who worked in the Russian lands from about the 1370s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theophanu-abbess-of-essen"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theophanu-abbess-of-essen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Theophanu, Abbess of Essen</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Theophanu was abbess of Essen from 1039 until her death in 1058.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/theophilos-emperor"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theophilos-emperor.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" he